        {
          "type": "rubric",
          "source": "Hymn - Norbert’s Penance",
          "english": null,
          "sourcePages": [
            573
          ],
          "translation": {
            "kind": "source-English",
            "sourcePages": [
              573
            ]
          },
          "alignment": "single-language",
          "id": "prime-b0006",
          "verification": "visual-review"
        },
        {
          "type": "prayer",
          "source": "With deep affliction thou hast mourned The failings of thy former life; Thy robes of princely state are spurned; The court, its pomps, its gilded strife, Are thine no more, for higher love Has fortified thy glowing heart, And, barefoot, through the snow thou’lt move As one all heedless of the smart Of scorn and insult. Then with fasts, With scourges, and with iron chain, Thou’lt seek to expiate the past, And heal, with care, the former pain Thy pride inflicted. May we now Rich fruit of penance daily bear, And in the hour of death do thou Obtain our pardon by thy prayer.",
          "english": null,
          "sourcePages": [
            573,
            574
          ],
          "translation": {
            "kind": "source-English",
            "sourcePages": [
              573,
              574
            ]
          },
          "alignment": "single-language",
          "id": "prime-b0007",
          "verification": "visual-review"
        },
        {
          "type": "rubric",
          "source": "Antiphon",
          "english": null,
          "sourcePages": [
            574
          ],
          "translation": {
            "kind": "source-English",
            "sourcePages": [
              574
            ]
          },
          "alignment": "single-language",
          "id": "prime-b0008",
          "verification": "visual-review"
        },
        {
          "type": "prayer",
          "source": "Having laid aside his store of earthly goods, and bestowed them upon the poor of Christ, denying himself, and following Christ in a narrower path, he went barefoot, putting on a hair shirt – the garb of a penitent.",
          "english": null,
          "sourcePages": [
            574
          ],
          "translation": {
            "kind": "source-English",
            "sourcePages": [
              574
            ]
          },
          "alignment": "single-language",
          "id": "prime-b0009",
          "verification": "visual-review"
        },
        {
